# 6. BIOMETRIC VERIFICATION — CORE PRODUCT MODULE

Biometrics are part of the ICE product vision. Do not treat this as an empty future placeholder.

Design a dedicated biometric verification module with these capabilities:

1. Selfie capture.
2. Face detection and image quality checks.
3. Liveness detection.
4. Presentation-attack detection.
5. One-to-one face comparison between the ID portrait and the captured selfie.
6. Explainable processing results and confidence/score handling.
7. Configurable thresholds and policy decisions.
8. Reviewer escalation when results are inconclusive.

Keep liveness detection and face comparison as separate checks.

Do not implement one-to-many face identification or cross-tenant face searching.

The module must include:
- Consent recording.
- Purpose limitation.
- Encryption.
- Strict access controls.
- Retention and deletion policies.
- Processing status.
- Model and version tracking.
- Audit events.
- A non-biometric alternative where appropriate.

Do not invent biometric accuracy percentages.

Do not claim certification, production-grade liveness or resistance to all spoofing attacks without independent evaluation.

Build an engine adapter architecture so biometric providers or locally deployed models can be integrated without redesigning the application.

The first implementation must provide the real capture workflow, consent handling, data model, secure processing boundaries and result states. If no validated liveness or face-comparison engine is configured, show NOT_CONFIGURED or NOT_PERFORMED. Never manufacture a successful biometric result.

Do not use face similarity alone to automatically determine access to financial or other high-impact services.

